1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 In this lesson, we're going to compare TypeScript directly with JavaScript.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 Now, a lot of new developers wonder, is TypeScript a totally different language?

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 Or is it just JavaScript with just a bit of extra fluff added on top?

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 So, the first thing you need to know is TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript,

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 and I've already spoken to you back that in a previous lesson.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 So, as a recap, this just means that every valid JavaScript file is also a valid TypeScript file.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 So, if you already know JavaScript, then you already know 90% of TypeScript.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 But TypeScript doesn't replace JavaScript, it builds functionality on top of it.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 The main difference between JavaScript and TypeScript is its type checking.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 JavaScript is dynamically typed. That's important to remember, is dynamically typed,

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 whereas TypeScript is statically typed.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 JavaScript only finds typed errors when the code actually runs,

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 whereas TypeScript finds our typed errors while we're writing the code.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 And that's one huge difference that has a big effect on reliability and developer productivity.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 Basically, consider as a metaphor, JavaScript equals seatbelt off,

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 whereas TypeScript equals seatbelt on.

17
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 Now, one thing that's also to bear in mind is TypeScript gets removed.

18
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 It actually compiles to JavaScript.

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 There is something that most beginners don't realise that TypeScript never runs in the browser.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 The browser actually has no idea what TypeScript is, and it cannot execute it.

21
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 The TypeScript code must be compiled into plain JavaScript.

22
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 And during that process, TypeScript removes all types, checks for errors,

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 and outputs clean JavaScript that any browser or Node.js environments can run.

24
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 So the flow basically looks like this. First, we write TypeScript,

25
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 then TypeScript checks our code, then TypeScript outputs normal JavaScript,

26
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 and finally the browser runs that JavaScript as per normal.

27
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 So TypeScript is just a developer tool, whereas JavaScript is actually what runs.

28
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 Now, another difference is that TypeScript introduces features that JavaScript doesn't support natively,

29
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
 or at least not yet. For example, things like interfaces, inums,

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 type aliases, access modifiers like public and private generics, and decorators.

31
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 Now, these features help teams manage complexity as the applications grow much larger.

32
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 There's some of these features eventually get adopted into JavaScript itself.

33
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 So in a way, TypeScript helps push the language forward.

34
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 Now, one of the things the developers absolutely love about TypeScript is the tooling.

35
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 In JavaScript, your editor basically guesses what types your variables might have.

36
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 But in TypeScript, your editor actually knows.

37
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 That means that we have more accurate autocompletion.

38
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 We have better error messages. We have the ability to safely refactor our code,

39
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 and we also get real-time documentation and type hints.

40
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 And this basically means that we can write code faster and with a lot more confidence.

41
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 Now, another question you might hear is does TypeScript actually make our app slower?

42
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 And the answer is no. TypeScript has zero impact on how our code actually runs.

43
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 It's completely removed before execution.

44
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 Once it's compiled, which runs in a browser, is just optimised JavaScript.

45
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 So when can we use JavaScript and when should you use TypeScript?

46
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 For QuickScripts, or perhaps toy, throw out projects, or when doing project types,

47
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 or just learning JS concepts, then JavaScript is totally fine.

48
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 They have anything serious, especially if apps that's going to grow over dozens of files,

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 or involve multiple with the variables, then TypeScript is almost always going to be the better choice.

50
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 Most modern projects, frameworks and libraries, are now built with TypeScript by default.

51
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:54,000
 In summary, TypeScript is basically JavaScript plus types plus better tooling plus advanced features that makes our code safer and easier to maintain.

52
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 We still write JavaScript, but TypeScript just makes our entire development process so much smoother and far less error-prone.

